### What changes with the Hibernate dialect?

Hibernate generates database-specific SQL through its dialect. On MySQL, it uses LIMIT for pagination and AUTO_INCREMENT for identifiers. On Oracle, it uses FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY (or ROWNUM on older versions) and SEQUENCE for identifiers. Changing the dialect flips all generated SQL.

```yaml
# Before: MySQL
spring:
  jpa:
    database-platform: org.hibernate.dialect.MySQLDialect
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:mysql://db-host:3306/bankdb
    driver-class-name: com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver

# After: Oracle
spring:
  jpa:
    database-platform: org.hibernate.dialect.OracleDialect
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:oracle:thin:@//db-host:1521/ORCLPDB1
    driver-class-name: oracle.jdbc.OracleDriver
```

```text
Pagination SQL difference:
  MySQL:  SELECT * FROM accounts LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0
  Oracle: SELECT * FROM accounts FETCH FIRST 20 ROWS ONLY

Hibernate handles this automatically when the dialect is set correctly.
```

### What changes with the JDBC driver and URL?

Replace the MySQL Connector/J Maven dependency with the Oracle JDBC driver and change the URL format.

```xml
<!-- Before: MySQL -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.mysql</groupId>
    <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
</dependency>

<!-- After: Oracle -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.oracle.database.jdbc</groupId>
    <artifactId>ojdbc11</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```text
URL format:
  MySQL:  jdbc:mysql://host:3306/database
  Oracle: jdbc:oracle:thin:@//host:1521/service_name

Port:
  MySQL:  3306 (default)
  Oracle: 1521 (default)
```

### What changes with identifier generation?

MySQL AUTO_INCREMENT uses GenerationType.IDENTITY, which prevents batch inserts because Hibernate needs each id immediately. Oracle uses SEQUENCE objects. Switch to GenerationType.SEQUENCE with a @SequenceGenerator, and create the sequence in the Oracle schema. As a bonus, sequences enable batch inserts.

```java
// Before: MySQL (IDENTITY / auto-increment)
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
}
```

```java
// After: Oracle (SEQUENCE)
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "acc_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "acc_seq", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
}
```

```sql
-- Oracle DDL: create the sequence
CREATE SEQUENCE account_seq START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 50;
```

```text
Benefit of switching to SEQUENCE:
- IDENTITY: 1 round trip per INSERT (no batching)
- SEQUENCE: 1 round trip per 50 IDs (batching works!)
- Migration to Oracle actually IMPROVES insert performance
```

### What are the schema and type differences?

Oracle has different data types and behaviours that cause subtle runtime surprises.

| MySQL Type | Oracle Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| VARCHAR(n) | VARCHAR2(n) | Hibernate dialect handles mapping |
| DECIMAL(p,s) | NUMBER(p,s) | BigDecimal maps correctly to both |
| BOOLEAN | NUMBER(1) | Older Oracle has no native BOOLEAN |
| TEXT | CLOB | Large text storage |
| BLOB | BLOB | Binary data |
| AUTO_INCREMENT | SEQUENCE | Must create sequence objects |
| '' (empty string) | NULL | Oracle treats '' as NULL |

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "acc_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "acc_seq", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "holder_name", nullable = false, length = 100)
    private String holderName;     // VARCHAR2(100) on Oracle

    @Column(name = "balance", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;    // NUMBER(15,2) on Oracle

    @Column(name = "active")
    private boolean active;        // NUMBER(1) on Oracle: 0 or 1

    @Column(name = "notes", columnDefinition = "CLOB")
    private String notes;          // CLOB on Oracle
}
```

### What about the empty-string-is-null problem?

Oracle treats an empty string ('') as NULL. Code or queries that rely on distinguishing an empty string from null will behave differently. This is a common source of bugs after migration.

```java
// WRONG: relying on empty string being different from null
package com.bank.service;

public class SearchService {
    public boolean hasMiddleName(String middleName) {
        return middleName != null && !middleName.isEmpty();
        // On MySQL: middleName = "" -> returns false (correct)
        // On Oracle: middleName stored as '' -> stored as NULL
        //           middleName retrieved as null -> returns false
        // But: queries like WHERE middle_name = '' will find NOTHING on Oracle
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: treat empty string and null the same way
package com.bank.service;

public class SearchService {
    public boolean hasMiddleName(String middleName) {
        return middleName != null && !middleName.isBlank();
        // Works on both MySQL and Oracle
    }
}
```

```text
Oracle empty-string-is-null:
  INSERT INTO accounts (holder_name, middle_name) VALUES ('Rajesh', '')
  -> middle_name is stored as NULL in Oracle
  -> SELECT * WHERE middle_name = '' returns NO rows
  -> SELECT * WHERE middle_name IS NULL returns the row
```

### What about Oracle identifier case sensitivity?

Oracle folds unquoted identifiers to UPPERCASE. If your Hibernate naming strategy produces lowercase or mixed-case names, they become uppercase in Oracle. Quoted identifiers are case-sensitive, so avoid mixing quoted and unquoted names.

```java
// Table name in entity
@Table(name = "accounts")   // Oracle: unquoted -> ACCOUNTS
@Table(name = "\"Accounts\"")  // Oracle: quoted -> Accounts (exact case)
// Keep it simple: use lowercase unquoted names, let Oracle fold to uppercase
```

```text
Oracle case rules:
  Unquoted: accounts -> ACCOUNTS (uppercase)
  Quoted:   "Accounts" -> Accounts (exact case, case-sensitive)
  Best practice: use lowercase unquoted names consistently
```

### How do you handle the BOOLEAN type?

Older Oracle has no native BOOLEAN column type. A Java boolean field maps to NUMBER(1) through the Hibernate dialect or an AttributeConverter. Oracle 23c adds a real BOOLEAN type, but for compatibility use a converter.

```java
package com.bank.converter;

import jakarta.persistence.AttributeConverter;
import jakarta.persistence.Converter;

@Converter(autoApply = true)
public class BooleanToNumberConverter implements AttributeConverter<Boolean, Integer> {

    @Override
    public Integer convertToDatabaseColumn(Boolean value) {
        return value != null && value ? 1 : 0;
    }

    @Override
    public Boolean convertToEntityAttribute(Integer value) {
        return value != null && value == 1;
    }
}
```

```text
Java: boolean active = true
Oracle: NUMBER(1) = 1
Java: boolean active = false
Oracle: NUMBER(1) = 0
```

### What is the migration process?

Run the migration in stages. First, make the application dialect/driver-agnostic by removing MySQL-specific native queries. Then switch the dialect, driver, and identifier strategy. Finally, run the full regression against a real Oracle instance.

```yaml
# Stage 3: validate schema against Oracle
spring:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: validate    # verify entity mappings match Oracle schema
    database-platform: org.hibernate.dialect.OracleDialect
```

```text
Migration stages:
1. Audit native queries for MySQL-only syntax (LIMIT, MySQL functions)
2. Rewrite native queries to be portable or Oracle-compatible
3. Switch dialect, driver, URL in config
4. Switch IDENTITY to SEQUENCE in entities
5. Create Oracle sequences and DDL
6. Run Flyway/Liquibase with Oracle-compatible migrations
7. Set ddl-auto: validate to verify mappings
8. Run full regression + integration tests against real Oracle
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer audits every native query for MySQL-only constructs before switching. They test the empty-string-is-null behaviour on all NOT NULL varchar columns. They keep identifier naming consistent to avoid Oracle's case-sensitivity issues. They set ddl-auto to validate against the real Oracle schema.

```java
// Senior checklist before migration:
// 1. Audit @Query(nativeQuery=true) for MySQL-specific SQL
// 2. Replace LIMIT with Pageable (Hibernate handles FETCH FIRST)
// 3. Replace AUTO_INCREMENT with SEQUENCE
// 4. Test empty string vs null on all String columns
// 5. Add BooleanToNumberConverter for boolean fields
// 6. Run integration tests against Oracle Docker container
```
